            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "hironda"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I have used this for years. I have played around with it a little and found that a teaspoon of sugar cuts the acidity of the tomatoes. And if you want to make it taste a little richer, stir in a quarter cup of cream before serving. Also, I drain grease off the beef before adding any vegetables, that way you aren't pouring out all your garlic/onion flavour!"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "glenda"
            },
            "reviewBody": "We used this recipe for a benefit where we fed over 400 people, everyone enjoyed it. We increased the ingredients by 500, did the math, added a little more beef than what it called for, but it turned out great and was fairly easy to do. If I am crazy enough to do another benefit, we will use this recipe.",
            "datePublished": "2009-10-11T18:25:08.740Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "elinor3"
            },
            "reviewBody": "This recipe was very easy and tasty. It's a really hearty sauce and next time I'll add sauteed mushrooms. I'll certainly make it again!!",
            "datePublished": "2009-04-26T18:46:25.970Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "morin3"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I fixed this for supper tonight and it went over wonderful. Wife even went back for a second helping even though she doesn’t like tomatoes. The best spaghetti sauce I have ever eaten. I began by browning the ground beef, onion, garlic, and green pepper in a sauce pan. While the meat and vegetables where browning, I combined the diced tomatoes, tomato sauce, and tomato paste in the crock pot. Once the meat and vegetables where brown, I added the oregano, basil, salt, and pepper and let that simmer for a short time. I then combined all the ingredients in the crock pot and let it simmer for four hours.",
            "datePublished": "2010-03-16T17:54:06.920Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Robin R"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Great recipe! I added some cream (1/2 c.), added some mild Italian sausage and used red bell pepper instead of green. I added 1/2 t. of sugar also. I cooked the meat, then removed it from the pan with a slotted spoon and cooked the veggies in about 2 T. of the grease, then added the meat back in. If you do add Italian sausage, remember to greatly reduce the amount of salt to maybe 1/8 t. or it will be too salty! Since all the stores were closed for Easter, and we couldn't get our Old Bay steamed snow crab, we had Easter spaghetti instead.",
            "datePublished": "2020-04-12T23:48:42.080Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "claire"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Easy to make because I usually have these ingredients in my kitchen. I added 2 T. soy sauce, 1/4 t. salt, 1/2 t. sugar and 2 t. Worcestershire sauce to the ground beef before I sautéed it with the onions and garlic, as another reader advised. I do not like green peppers, so did not add them, but did add a small can of sliced olives. Also, I like my sauce a bit spicy, so I added a few shakes of red pepper flakes. I put it all in my crock pot on high for 3 hours. My husband and I loved it! Thank you!!",
            "datePublished": "2018-09-28T22:41:04.147Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Lara Kaljumaa"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Easy and delicious! Husband saw all the peppers I was putting in and looked very skeptical, but ended up saying he liked it and couldn't taste them. I did add a few extras: increased the sugar to about 1/4 cup, 8 oz can of mushroom pieces, 1/4 t. of fennel seed, a splash of Worcestershire sauce and red wine vinegar, and sprinkle of crushed red pepper. I sauteed the garlic in a bit of olive oil along with the spices before adding in the cooked hamburger, onion, and pepper. 5 stars for flavor and simplicity. Update: made it again - this time I sauteed the onions and peppers, added 1 T Italian seasoning instead of basil and oregano, a sprinkle of red pepper, salt and pepper and then pureed them briefly in a blender with some of the tomatoes. Added back into the pan with the rest of the tomatoes, about 2T of white sugar and a dash of red wine vinegar. Still delicious!",
            "datePublished": "2020-04-05T01:02:29.190Z"
          },
          {
